        question: "How do I know if my rotator cuff is torn?",
        answer: "The pattern I look for is weakness that is out of proportion to pain, especially when I test one tendon against resistance, plus a story of sudden loss of strength or long-standing trouble with overhead use. Classic signs include the arm dropping when you try to lower it slowly from shoulder height (a drop-arm test), weakness turning the arm outward against resistance, or difficulty lifting the arm overhead without hiking up the shoulder blade. Clinical tests are imperfect, so when knowing whether there is a tear would change the plan, I flag it to your family doctor or specialist and refer you for an ultrasound or MRI."
