    faqs: [
      {
        question: "Is piriformis syndrome the same as sciatica?",
        answer: "Not quite. Sciatica usually means nerve pain that starts at the spine, typically from a disc pressing on a nerve root. What used to be called piriformis syndrome is now better understood as deep gluteal syndrome: irritation of the sciatic nerve as it travels through the deep buttock, by the piriformis or by other structures in that space (Martin et al., J Hip Preserv Surg 2015). The symptoms can feel like sciatica, but the cause and the treatment differ."
      },
      {
        question: "How do I know if it is my piriformis and not a disc?",
        answer: "The typical pattern with deep gluteal or piriformis pain is deep buttock tenderness when the area is pressed, worse with long sitting, and often without much low back pain. A disc pressing on a nerve root more commonly causes back pain plus leg pain, is brought on by nerve tension tests (straight leg raise or slump), and may include weakness in the muscles that nerve supplies or a reflex change, although leg pain from a nerve root can also occur without much back pain. The history and examination can suggest whether symptoms are more likely to come from the back, the deep buttock or another source. Sometimes the pattern stays uncertain and needs reassessment or medical investigation. Imaging is kept for cases with warning signs (red flags) or when non-surgical care has not worked."
      },
      {
        question: "How long does piriformis syndrome take to settle?",
        answer: "Many people improve over several weeks with a combination of load management, graded hip strengthening and sensible changes to sitting, but recovery times vary and the research on them is limited. Long-standing cases, or cases where fitness has dropped a lot, can take longer, which is one reason addressing it early helps."
      },
      {
        question: "Should I stretch my piriformis aggressively?",
        answer: "No. Hard stretching to the limit often irritates a nerve that is already sensitive and makes things worse. Gentle, brief stretches that do not bring on leg symptoms, combined with nerve gliding (gentle movements that slide the nerve) and strengthening of the muscles that turn the hip outward and the glutes, tend to help more than repeatedly pulling the knee to the opposite shoulder."
      },
      {
        question: "Why does sitting make it so much worse?",
        answer: "Sitting squeezes the deep buttock space between the sitting bone and the seat, and stretches the piriformis when the hip is bent, drawn across the body and turned inward, which is how many people sit. For sudden flares I recommend standing or walking breaks every 20 to 30 minutes, avoiding cross-legged sitting, and using a firm cushion that takes weight off the painful side."
      },
      {
        question: "Can I run with piriformis syndrome?",
        answer: "Often yes, with changes, provided running does not consistently bring on or worsen leg symptoms. Short, flat, gentle-paced runs are usually tolerated before long runs or hills. What tends to flare it is long sitting before or after a run, hard hill work, and sudden increases in distance. A gradual walk-run return usually works better than pushing through."
      },
      {
        question: "Do I need a cortisone injection or surgery?",
        answer: "Surgery for deep gluteal syndrome is uncommon and kept for a trapped nerve clearly shown on imaging that has not improved with sustained non-surgical care. Piriformis injections guided by imaging (local anaesthetic, with or without corticosteroid) are sometimes used when symptoms are severe or the diagnosis is unclear, but many people improve with non-surgical care without them."
      },
      {
        question: "Do I need an MRI?",
        answer: "Not for most cases. The diagnosis is made from your history and examination: the lower back examines normally, and specific tests that load the deep buttock bring on the pain. MRI is kept for warning signs (red flags), when non-surgical care is not progressing, or when it is still unclear what else it could be. Ultrasound with an experienced operator sometimes helps when planning a targeted injection."
      },
      {
        question: "What actually compresses the nerve if it is not always the piriformis?",
        answer: "The deep buttock space has several possible culprits: the piriformis in some people whose anatomy differs, fibrous bands, a group of small deep hip muscles (the gemelli and obturator internus), the top of the hamstring, and blood vessels. Hernando et al. (Skeletal Radiology 2015) mapped this thoroughly. Part of the assessment is working out which structure is most likely in your case so the loading plan targets the right one."
      }
    ],

