    faqs: [
      {
        question: "How long before I am back playing?",
        answer: "It depends on the size of the tear. In a rehab study of male athletes with new inner thigh (adductor) injuries who progressed by meeting set criteria (Serner et al., Orthop J Sports Med 2020), those with small to moderate injuries on MRI returned to full team training after about 3 weeks, and most with a complete tear returned within 3 months. The criteria matter more than the calendar. Returning when pain-free but still weak is a common reason groin strains come back."
      },
      {
        question: "Is this a pulled muscle or a sports hernia?",
        answer: "Different problems, and they need different plans. A classic adductor strain is tender right where the main inner thigh muscle attaches to the pelvis (the adductor longus origin), and squeezing the legs together against resistance brings on the pain. What is often called a sports hernia (inguinal-related groin pain in the Doha classification) sits deeper, around the groin crease (the inguinal canal), often flares with coughing, sneezing or sit-ups, and is not clearly brought on by a simple squeeze test. A true hernia, a lump that bulges with coughing or straining, is a different problem again and needs your family doctor's assessment. Serner et al. (BJSM 2016) showed that examination matches imaging well for adductor injuries but misses more with other causes of groin pain, which is why history and exam have to work together."
      },
      {
        question: "Should I rest or should I train?",
        answer: "Rest the first two or three days while things are acutely painful. After that, pain-free loading beats continued rest. The Hölmich protocol showed decades ago that active training returned athletes with long-standing adductor pain to sport at a much higher rate than passive physiotherapy (treatment done to you rather than exercise), and the same early-loading pattern shows up across more recent guidelines."
      },
      {
        question: "What is the Copenhagen adduction exercise and do I have to do it?",
        answer: "It is a side-lying exercise, with the top leg supported by a partner or a bench, that loads the inner thigh muscles (adductors) with the leg held long, as they lengthen under control (eccentric work). In a trial of male footballers (Harøy et al., BJSM 2019), teams that did it three times a week in preseason and once a week in season had a 41 percent lower risk of players reporting groin problems over the season. I build it into almost every groin rehab and leave it in as long-term prevention for anyone in sports with kicking or sudden changes of direction."
      },
      {
        question: "Why does it still hurt weeks later?",
        answer: "Usually one of three things: the load was increased too fast, the loading plan was too light (light bands for a kicking athlete is not enough), or the problem is not just an adductor strain. Pain lasting beyond six to eight weeks is a reason to re-examine, and sometimes to refer you through your doctor for imaging. Long-standing cases often combine adductor-related groin pain with a second problem, such as pain from the pubic bone, which changes the program."
      },
      {
        question: "Do I need an MRI?",
        answer: "Not for most new adductor strains. The diagnosis is made from your history and examination, and imaging rarely changes the plan in the first few weeks. I cannot order imaging myself, so I flag it to your family doctor or a sports medicine physician and refer you when imaging would change the plan: a high-grade injury with extensive bruising, when a tendon or piece of bone may have pulled away (an avulsion), when symptoms fail to settle with appropriate loading, or when the history points to the groin crease (inguinal) area or the pubic bone rather than a pure adductor strain."
      },
      {
        question: "Can I prevent this happening again?",
        answer: "You can lower the risk. Two common contributors to it coming back are incomplete rehab (returning before strength and equal hops on both legs are back) and dropping adductor loading during the season. Keep the Copenhagen adduction exercise in at least once a week through the season, keep your inner thigh strength up (in one study of professional ice hockey players, adductor strength below 80 percent of outer hip (abductor) strength was linked to more strains), and warm up properly before kicking sessions."
      }
    ],

