        answer: "It can. Arthritis after an injury (post-traumatic arthritis) or bone loss at the end of the collarbone (distal clavicular osteolysis) can develop years later, particularly in people who press heavily or do a lot of overhead work. It is not known whether rehabilitation changes that long-term risk, but building shoulder strength and returning to heavy pressing gradually can help the joint cope with the demands you put on it. If pain at the top of the shoulder comes back, it is worth having it reassessed."
