    faqs: [
      {
        question: "How is SI joint pain actually diagnosed?",
        answer: "From the history and examination, by looking at several tests that try to bring on the pain (distraction, thigh thrust, compression, sacral thrust) together, alongside an examination of the back and hips, rather than relying on a single test. Laslett and colleagues (Manual Therapy 2005) found that combinations of these tests matched diagnostic injections (numbing injections into the joint) better than single tests. A 2021 meta-analysis (Saueressig et al., JOSPT) rated the evidence as very low certainty: a positive group of tests is not enough to confirm the SI joint as the source, while a negative group makes it much less likely. Positive tests also do not by themselves predict how well rehabilitation will go. Imaging rarely confirms the source, so the history and exam usually drive the working diagnosis."
      },
      {
        question: "Is my SI joint actually out of place?",
        answer: "Almost certainly not in the way that is often described. The SI joint moves only around 2 to 4 millimetres and 2 to 4 degrees, and no reliable hands-on test detects small changes in position. What people feel as \"out\" is typically changed movement, muscles guarding the area, and tenderness, all of which respond to load and training rather than to repeated adjustment."
      },
      {
        question: "Why does pregnancy make SI pain so common?",
        answer: "Hormonal changes loosen the ligaments around the pelvis, and the growing baby shifts the body's centre of mass forward, which together load the SI joint differently. Pelvic girdle pain is common in pregnancy. Most cases settle after the birth, particularly when glute and trunk strength is rebuilt with a graded program."
      },
      {
        question: "How long does SI joint pain usually take to settle?",
        answer: "Many people improve over 6 to 8 weeks with a combination of hands-on treatment and graded strengthening of the deep trunk and buttock (gluteal) muscles, although some take longer. It is not uncommon for it to come back in the first year, particularly when load drops off, which is why a maintenance program matters as much as the initial rehab."
      },
      {
        question: "Is a pelvic support belt a good idea?",
        answer: "Short-term, yes, for many people. A sacroiliac or pelvic belt can reduce symptoms during activities that reliably flare the joint, particularly in pregnancy and soon after the birth. It is a tool for the early phase rather than a long-term solution. Over time, the belt is standing in for the firm hold the glutes and trunk muscles give the joint (force closure)."
      },
      {
        question: "Can I run or lift with SI joint pain?",
        answer: "Often yes, with targeted changes. Exercises that load one side more than the other, like single-leg deadlifts, lunges and step-ups, sometimes flare SI symptoms early on, so in the first phase I often shift the program toward hinging at the hips on both legs, bridging and trunk work. As symptoms settle, single-leg and impact loading come back in step by step."
      },
      {
        question: "Do I need an SI joint injection or surgery?",
        answer: "Surgery (SI joint fusion) is uncommon and reserved for carefully selected persistent cases, after specialist assessment and failure of appropriate nonsurgical care. Selection involves more than imaging and may include diagnostic injections. Diagnostic and therapeutic injections are sometimes used when the diagnosis is unclear or symptoms are severe, but they sit alongside, not instead of, structured rehabilitation."
      }
    ],

